    title: Icosahedron Virus
    description: Spherical viruses have round shapes, like balls. They have a protein
      shell or outer cover that's super symmetric to protect their inner parts, like
      in adenovirus, poliovirus, and measles. They aren't always perfect spheres
      but look neat and tidy.
  - image: {fileID: -6059336636965133244, guid: ed6f161d4d6e3ca448b148670eb518f5, type: 3}
    title: Mycoplasma
    description: Mycoplasma are special bacteria with no outer wall. They are the
      tiniest free-living germs, super small at just 0.2 to 0.3 micrometers. So tiny
      they slip through filters that catch other bacteria.
  - image: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 217e4d3837569f447a73f61da185a57b, type: 3}
    title: Immune Cell
    description: 'Immune cells, also called white blood cells, act like soldiers
      in your body. They travel through your blood and tissues to spot and fight
      bad germs like viruses and bacteria.

      In this patient, the immune cells
      don''t work right and can''t kill bacteria alone, so they call for cytokines
      to help.'
  - image: {fileID: 21300000, guid: a593654a343828f4db55ca447875bd65, type: 3}
    title: Cytokine
    description: "Interferon gamma, or IFN-\u03B3, is a strong message protein called
      a cytokine. It turns on your immune system's strongest fighters and is key
      for killing bacteria hiding inside cells, unlike other interferons that mostly
      battle viruses.\nIn our patient, the gammas are broken and make the immune
      cells way too active."
  uiAudioSource: {fileID: 400523131}
  navSfx: {fileID: 8300000, guid: 41b1dff1d67040e4dae6bd4f7ceb1ee2, type: 3}
  closeSfx: {fileID: 8300000, guid: a9aed93da3a6c814f83507b737396685, type: 3}
